# Code organization

## Organize by the reader's task

A folder or file exists because it owns something a reader would ask
about. What journey is this. What stage of that journey. What does this
file do inside that stage. Name things by that answer, not by the
language object inside them. Prefer `parse-row.ts` over `fns.ts`, and
`rank-results.ts` over `helpers.ts`.

## Deep modules

A good module has a small simple public surface and hides the real work
behind it. The narrowness of the interface is the main quality metric.
Line count is a hint, not a target.

Prefer one cohesive longer file with one clear public function over
several tiny files that are always imported together. Splitting cohesive
code into category files makes shallow modules: lots of surface, little
hidden, and too much jumping for the reader.

Defaults:

- Split when the public surface grows two unrelated concerns, not when a
  byte count crosses a line.
- Keep helpers private while they serve one module.
- Promote a helper to shared only after a second real caller appears.
- Delete structure that has stopped paying for itself.

## Module shape

A module is a folder. It has one top-level `index.ts` (or `index.tsx`)
that is its public door. Everything else in the module is a nested,
named file, not another `index`. The reader opens `index` to learn what
the module offers, then follows the names inward.

- `types.ts` exists only when several files in the module share the same
  types. If one file owns a type, the type lives next to it.
- No `fns`, `fx`, `literals`, `utils`, `helpers`, or `shared` files.
  Those are syntax buckets, not modules. Name files for what they do.
- No barrel files that only re-export their siblings.

These are the default. A specific UI may set its own house rules in a
scoped rule file, but it starts from here.

## React component files

Default to one React component per file. A component file owns its main
capitalized component and may keep private render helpers that are not
components. A second capitalized component is the signal to split — never
let unrelated components pile up in one file.

The split rule is simple: **the moment one thing needs a second file, it
becomes a folder.** A lone component is a file (`foo.tsx`). The instant it
needs a helper component — a second file for the same thing — turn it into
a module: `foo/index.tsx` (the public door a consumer imports) plus the
helper as a named sibling *inside* that folder (`foo/helper.tsx`). Never
scatter the helper as a loose file in the feature root next to the parent;
group it under the thing it serves. One component per file still holds —
the folder is just the grouping.

This holds at any count, not just large clusters — two files is already a
folder. So a chat view that grew a pill, a streamed-text block, a timer,
and an assistant turn becomes `chat-view/` with `index.tsx` (the view),
`message-pill.tsx`, `streamed.tsx`, `elapsed-timer.tsx`, and
`assistant-block.tsx` — never loose files in the feature root, and never
one 300-line file.

Whether to split at all is still governed by cohesion (see "Deep modules"
and the escalation list below): don't shatter a cohesive single component
into pieces just to make files. But once a second file is warranted, it is
a folder. The collapse rules run the other way — a folder that ends up
holding one real file folds back to a single file.

### Components in disguise count toward the split

The trigger above is "a second capitalized component", and that wording has
a loophole that has been exploited: writing the second component as a
lowercase JSX-returning helper (`const entryRow = (entry) => <Block …>`) or
as an inner `function sourcesFeedback()` hoisted below the return. Those ARE
components for this rule — they own a markup block, copy, and behavior. The
channels create-channel-modal shipped as one 370-line file this way and had
to be re-split in review into `create-channel-modal/` with `index.tsx`,
`source-rows.tsx`, and `problem-lines.ts`.

Concrete tripwires — when ANY of these hits, the file becomes a folder with
`index.tsx` plus named siblings, no matter how the units are spelled:

- a second JSX-returning unit beyond a trivial few-liner (a `metaNote`-sized
  one-liner is fine; an `entryRow` with its own layout and motion is not),
- a presentation table + its consumers growing past the component itself
  (status→look maps, action clusters → `looks.tsx`, `row-actions.tsx`),
- the file passing ~300 lines with distinct regions a reader scrolls between.

Don't be afraid of folders: siblings keep the module self-contained while
each file stays one readable unit. The one legitimate stay-inside case is a
hoisted `function` helper that closes over the component's live state and
would need a double-digit prop interface to extract — that's cohesion, not
sprawl. Everything else splits.

Inline SVG components are icons. Put reusable icon components under
`atoms/icons/<icon-name>/index.tsx` and export them from `atoms/icons`;
do not bury them inside a feature component.

## Build UI from atoms, not raw HTML + inline CSS

Feature UIs are assembled from the shared atoms (`Block`, `Button`, `Text`,
and the rest of `atoms/blocks`), never from bare `<div>` / `<button>` /
`<span>` carrying inline `style={{…}}` objects or a `*.module.css` file. The
atoms are Panda components: static styling is written as props
(`<Block position="absolute" pr={18} borderRadius="6px">`), so the value
lives on the component instead of in a detached style object or stylesheet.

- `Block` for layout and containers, `Text` for runs of text, `Button` for
  anything clickable. Reach for these even for a one-off box.
- Keep `style={{…}}` only for values that are genuinely dynamic per render —
  a computed `left` / `top` / `width`, a transition string built from props.
  Everything static (position, z-index, transform-origin, radius, colors) is
  a prop, not a style entry. Hover/focus rules go on `_hover` / `_focus`.
- No `*.module.css` files for feature UI, and no CSS classes hand-wired with
  `className={styles.x}`. Panda already owns the styling layer; port the rules
  onto the atom.
- The one raw DOM element that's still correct is a node an imperative library
  demands (e.g. a MapLibre `Marker` element made with `document.createElement`).
  That isn't UI you render through React — leave it as a DOM node.

## Imports

Inside a module, import the exact sibling file and the exact symbols you
use, with a relative path: `import { parseRow } from "./parse-row"`.

Across a module boundary, import through the public door using a path
alias. An alias reads as two parts: a prefix that says what kind of
module this is, and a leaf that names the module, so `<prefix>/<name>`.
Cross-module imports are namespace imports so the call site stays
uniform: `import * as Playback from "platform/playback"`, then
`Playback.start(...)`. Type-only imports take the same shape with
`import type * as ...`.

Each UI defines its own prefixes. Never reach across a boundary with a
deep relative path like `../../other-module/internal`.

**An alias is exactly two segments — `<prefix>/<name>` — never three.** To
reach something inside a module, import its door and take the member off it:
destructure (`import { useColumnLayout } from "v2/hooks"`) or namespace-import
(`import * as Sidebar from "v2/sidebar"`, then `Sidebar.Foo`). Never write a
third path segment like `v2/hooks/use-column-layout` — that is what the door
and the destructure are for. The module's `index` re-exports its public
surface; the only exception is a heavy sub-module that would bloat the door
(e.g. `atoms/blocks/table-v2`), imported directly.

Current prefixes: `atoms/*` (the design system — its own `blocks`/`icons`/
`hooks` leaves), `entities/*` (the shared data / service layer), `panda/*`
(Panda codegen), and `v2/*` → `src/uis/atlas/*` (the Atlas UI's feature modules
plus its `parts` / `hooks` spine). `cells/*` and `features/*` belong to the
original UI only — Atlas never imports them.

## Feature shapes

Use the smallest shape that explains the work.

- Simple leaf: one cohesive file. Types, constants, and private helpers
  live next to the flow they support.
- Workflow: an ordered flow becomes stage files with a short
  orchestrator that reads like a story. Each stage owns its own mess.
- Capability: a long-lived service with no strict order exposes a small
  public contract and hides its internal files.
- Named states: when flags must change together, model named states and
  transitions, not parallel booleans.

## When to grow a file into a folder

Escalate when any of these is true:

- The file owns two genuinely unrelated concerns.
- The file has a clear internal sequence of three or more named stages.
- A surface has more than one layout or mode.
- An update function has many variants that cluster by behavior.
- A coordinator returns so many fields that callers need named
  capabilities.

Cohesion beats line count. A long file that does one well-named thing
can stay.

## When to collapse a folder

De-escalate, and do it aggressively, when:

- Sibling files are almost always edited together.
- A wrapper only forwards to another function.
- A folder holds one meaningful file.
- A helper is used once and adds no domain name.
- A file exists only because code was sorted by syntax.

Indirection is a cost even when each file is short.

## Side-effect boundaries

Keep pure logic apart from input and output. A pure module takes plain
values in and returns plain values out. It does not import framework,
network, filesystem, database, or rendering tools. Side-effect modules
sit at the edges and translate between the outside world and the pure
core. You should see the boundary from the imports alone. If a pure
module needs an I/O type in its signature, the boundary is leaking.

## Data shape discipline

Prefer shapes that encode the rules over loose maps and booleans.

- Give one concept one representation across a flow.
- When values travel together or change together, put them in one named
  object.
- Use enums or tagged unions for lifecycle, so impossible combinations
  like `loading && success` cannot be built.

## Pipelines

A long async flow has a short orchestrator and named steps. The
orchestrator tells the story in order. Each step owns its own edge
cases, retries, parsing, and cleanup. If every exception starts piling
up in the orchestrator, move that step's mess into the step. Name step
files for the stage they perform, never `helpers` or `utils`.

## Naming

### `state` is a BANNED word — never put it in an identifier

This is the rule most often missed, so it leads the section and is
repeated here on top of `typescript-style.mdc`. The word `state` may not
appear in any name **you** define — variables, parameters, fields,
methods, type aliases, interfaces, the setter you destructure from
`useState`, Rust structs/enums. It is a hard ban, not a style nicety.

Banned: `weatherState`, `WeatherState`, `columnState`, `TableState`,
`MenuState`, `getColumnState`, `setVolumeState`, `getInitialState`,
`ProducerState`, `state: Mutex<…>`. Every value in a program is "state",
so the word names nothing. Describe the actual shape instead —
`WeatherFeed`, `columnLayout`, `sortModel`, `selection`, `dragSession`,
`hoverInfo`.

The ONLY allowed `state` is an external API's own surface you cannot
rename (React's `useState` hook, YouTube's `PlayerState`). The value you
destructure from `useState` is OURS and is bound by the ban: write
`const [volume, storeVolume] = React.useState(0)`, never `setVolumeState`.

Full detail, more examples, and the Rust cases live in
`typescript-style.mdc` → "No `state` in identifiers".

### Other naming

Avoid `Use` prefixes and `T` prefixes on types (see `typescript-style.mdc`).
On top of those, avoid generic filler in any name you define: `data`,
`utils`, `helpers`, `shared`, `common`, `manager`, `handler`. Find the
real domain word instead. The only pass is a name an external library
already chose.

## No IIFEs

Do not wrap code in an immediately-called function like
`(() => { ... })()` or `(async () => { ... })()`. It hides the body
behind an anonymous wrapper the reader has to unwrap first. Use a named
function next to the call site, a top-level helper, a plain expression,
or a `switch` that returns a value. The only exception is a function
literal an external API asks you to pass, which is just a callback.

## Dates use `Temporal`, never `Date`

This is restated here (the detail lives in `typescript-style.mdc`) because it
keeps getting missed. `Date` is banned in the code we own — no `new Date`, no
`Date.now()`, none of its methods. Use `Temporal`, a polyfilled global already
wired in `atlas/index.tsx`: `Temporal.Now.instant().epochMilliseconds` for a
clock read, and a `Temporal.ZonedDateTime` (build it with the `localTime` /
`utcTime` helpers in `weather/format-time.ts`) to display an instant. The only
`Date` allowed is one an external library forces on us (e.g. `satellite.js`),
converted at that single boundary. The full swap table and examples live in
`typescript-style.mdc`.

## Failures and comments live elsewhere

Failures must surface with attribution and never get swallowed into a
normal-looking empty value. That rule lives in `no-silent-deferrals.mdc`
and `no-legacy-fallback-code.mdc`. How we write comments lives in
`documentation-comments.mdc`.
